// Garrison control plane — how a daemon proves it is itself.
//
// An Operator is a human with an Entra token. A garrison-agent daemon
// heartbeats at 03:00 with nobody logged in, so it cannot borrow that token
// and must carry an identity of its own. These two schemas are that identity,
// and neither of them stores a secret.
//
//   EnrollmentToken   — the bookkeeping for a signed, short-lived, single-use
//                       provisioning artifact minted outside this table.
//   InstallCredential — the standing identity: public verification material.
//
// The daemon generates its own keypair at enrollment and never transmits the
// private half; the plane records the public half and verifies signatures
// against it. A shared bearer secret would have been simpler and is
// deliberately absent: it would put a replayable credential in the database,
// on the wire at every heartbeat, and in a file on every workstation, which
// is three copies of a thing that only needs to exist in one place.
//
// The enrollment token follows the same rule, which took a second pass to get
// right. Modeled as a password — a random secret with its argon2 hash in the
// row — it was unmintable: the hash has to be @hidden, @hidden fields are
// rejected in request bodies, and a required field nobody may send cannot be
// created. Worse, the plaintext could never be returned to the human doing the
// provisioning, because a create response carries only persisted fields.
//
// So the artifact is not stored here at all. It is a PASETO v4 token minted
// with `schemaforge token generate` against the key the plane already holds,
// carrying the token id as `sub` and the grant as claims. Authenticity comes
// from the signature; this row supplies what a signature cannot — revocation,
// use counting, and the record of who issued it. Nothing secret is at rest,
// and the row is an ordinary entity any authorized console user can create.
//
// The trade, stated plainly: a database compromise still yields nothing, but
// a compromise of the signing key lets an attacker mint enrollment tokens.
// That key already protects every session token, so this concentrates no new
// trust — and the two are kept apart structurally rather than by convention.
// The two are kept apart by their role set, not by their issuer. acton-service
// validates `iss` against exactly one configured issuer, so an artifact minted
// under a second one is a 401 at the plane's own middleware and never reaches
// this service: both families are minted under `garrison-control-plane`. What
// separates them is that an artifact carries the single role `enrollee`, which
// `schemas/enrollment.schema` grants write on `Redemption` and nothing else
// anywhere in the bundle — an allowlist of one action rather than a credential
// merely refused everywhere else.
//
// The row records no issuer. It carried an `issuer` column until 1.0, compared
// against the configured issuer on every redemption, described as refusing a
// token minted for some other purpose against the same key. It could not do
// that: both sides are the same string in every deployment, because there is
// only one the plane accepts. What it caught was a typo in a hand-written row,
// which is not worth a `required` column on a surface that is about to freeze,
// and is much less than its doc comment promised a reader.
//
// Neither schema is deletable; see policies/custom/credential-lifecycle.cedar.
// A revoked credential is the evidence that it was revoked.

// The lifecycle of one provisioning grant. The signed artifact lives outside
// this row; what is here is everything a signature cannot express.
//
//   schemaforge token generate --sub tok_7f3a --lifetime 172800 \
//     --issuer garrison-control-plane --roles enrollee \
//     --tenant-chain '[{"schema":"Organization","entity_id":"<org id>"}]' \
//     --custom-claim-string scope=team \
//     --custom-claim-long max_uses=25
//
// The tenant chain is load-bearing: `_tenant` is injected from that claim and
// nothing else, so an artifact minted without it writes rows no tenant-scoped
// reader can see, including the hooks service that must adjudicate them.
//
// `--lifetime` is seconds, not a duration string; 172800 is the 48 hours a
// provisioning window should not outlive.
//
// Scope bounds the blast radius: an operator-scoped token provisions one
// person's machine, an organization-scoped token provisions anything that
// holds it. `expires_at` must be set to the same instant as the PASETO `exp`
// it describes — the token expires on its own, and this row is the copy a
// human can read.
@version(1)
@tenant(parent: "Organization")
@display("token_id")
@access(
    read:   ["auditor", "security_officer", "org_admin", "enrollment_service"],
    write:  ["security_officer", "org_admin", "enrollment_service"],
    delete: ["org_admin"]
)
schema EnrollmentToken {
    // The PASETO `sub`. Safe to log and safe to show in a list: it names the
    // grant without being the grant.
    token_id:          text(max: 64) required unique indexed @list(primary)

    organization:      -> Organization required

    scope:             enum("organization", "team", "operator")
                       required
                       @enum_colors(organization: "violet", team: "blue", operator: "teal")
                       @list(column)
    team:              -> Team
    operator:          -> Operator

    // What this grant mints. A durable grant admits a machine that keeps its
    // install record across restarts, which is every workstation. An
    // ephemeral one admits a machine that will not exist in ten minutes,
    // which is every CI runner.
    //
    // The distinction has to live on the grant rather than be inferred at
    // redemption, because the daemon cannot be the one to say it. A container
    // looks exactly like a first-run workstation from the plane's side: fresh
    // install id, fresh keypair, no record. Whoever provisions the runner
    // knows which it is, and this is where they say so.
    //
    // Orthogonal to `scope`, deliberately. Scope is blast radius, who this
    // grant may provision for. Lifecycle is what the resulting install is,
    // and a pipeline grant is usually operator-scoped and ephemeral at once.
    install_lifecycle: enum("durable", "ephemeral")
                       required
                       default("durable")
                       @default("'durable'")
                       @enum_colors(durable: "blue", ephemeral: "amber")
                       @list(column)

    // How long an install minted by this grant stays valid, in seconds.
    // Read only when `install_lifecycle` is ephemeral.
    //
    // It belongs to the grant rather than to the organization because build
    // length is a property of the pipeline: a lint job and a full integration
    // suite want very different numbers, and one organization runs both. The
    // default is an hour, which is longer than most builds and short enough
    // that a leaked packet buys an attacker an hour of one operator's seat
    // rather than a standing machine in the fleet.
    //
    // The ceiling is a day. A grant asking for more than that is describing a
    // durable install and should say so, so the honest thing is to refuse the
    // number rather than quietly mint something that outlives its pipeline.
    install_ttl_secs:  integer(min: 60, max: 86400)
                       default(3600)
                       @default("3600")
                       @require(
                           "install_lifecycle != 'ephemeral' || install_ttl_secs >= 60",
                           "an ephemeral enrollment grant must say how long the installs it mints may live"
                       )

    max_uses:          integer(min: 1) default(1) @default("1")
    uses:              integer(min: 0)
                       default(0)
                       @default("0")
                       @require(
                           "uses <= max_uses",
                           "an enrollment token cannot be redeemed more times than it allows"
                       )

    status:            enum("issued", "redeemed", "expired", "revoked")
                       default("issued")
                       @default("'issued'")
                       @enum_colors(issued: "blue", redeemed: "green", expired: "gray", revoked: "red")
                       @list(column)

    issued_at:         datetime @default("now")
    issued_by:         text(max: 320) required
    expires_at:        datetime required @format("relative")
    first_redeemed_at: datetime
    last_redeemed_at:  datetime
    revoked_at:        datetime

    revocation_reason: text(max: 512)
                       @default("''")
                       @require(
                           "status != 'revoked' || size(revocation_reason) > 0",
                           "revoking an enrollment token requires a recorded reason"
                       )

    installs:          -> AgentInstall[]
}

// The daemon's standing identity. One install may hold more than one row:
// during rotation the outgoing credential stays 'rotating' until the new one
// has been used, so a key roll does not require a maintenance window.
//
// Operators are absent from the read list on purpose. A daemon holds its own
// private key and needs nothing from this table; being able to enumerate the
// fleet's credentials is a capability it has no use for.
@version(1)
@tenant(parent: "Organization")
@display("credential_id")
@access(
    read:   ["team_lead", "auditor", "security_officer", "org_admin", "enrollment_service"],
    write:  ["security_officer", "org_admin", "enrollment_service"],
    delete: ["org_admin"]
)
schema InstallCredential {
    // The key id the daemon presents. Public by construction.
    credential_id:     text(max: 64) required unique indexed @list(primary)

    install:           -> AgentInstall required
    organization:      -> Organization required

    // Named to match the triplet a Redemption presents (`credential_kind`,
    // `public_key`, `cert_fingerprint`), so the hook copies fields across
    // without translating, and a wire reader sees one vocabulary.
    credential_kind:   enum("ed25519", "x509_mtls")
                       required
                       @enum_colors(ed25519: "teal", x509_mtls: "blue")
                       @list(column)

    // Base64 SPKI. For an mTLS credential this is the subject public key
    // carried by the certificate, so signature verification looks the same
    // either way and only the trust path differs.
    public_key:        text(max: 1024) required

    // SHA-256 of the DER certificate, which is what acton-service's client
    // certificate verification pins against. Meaningless for a bare keypair.
    cert_fingerprint:  text(max: 128)
                       indexed
                       @default("''")
                       @require(
                           "credential_kind != 'x509_mtls' || size(cert_fingerprint) == 64",
                           "an mTLS credential must record its certificate fingerprint"
                       )
    cert_serial:       text(max: 128)
    issuing_ca:        text(max: 255)

    status:            enum("pending", "active", "rotating", "revoked", "expired")
                       default("pending")
                       @default("'pending'")
                       @enum_colors(
                           pending: "gray",
                           active: "green",
                           rotating: "amber",
                           revoked: "red",
                           expired: "neutral"
                       )
                       @list(column)
                       // A retired install has no business holding a live key.
                       // Single-hop related read, evaluated against the
                       // committed install row.
                       @require(
                           "status != 'active' || related.install.status != 'retired'",
                           "a retired install cannot hold an active credential"
                       )

    // The credential this one supersedes. Self-referencing so a rotation
    // chain is walkable: every key back to enrollment, in order.
    supersedes:        -> InstallCredential

    issued_at:         datetime @default("now")
    expires_at:        datetime @format("relative")
    activated_at:      datetime
    last_used_at:      datetime @format("relative")
    // IPv6 textual maximum, so the column never silently truncates an address.
    last_used_from:    text(max: 45)
    use_count:         integer(min: 0) default(0)

    revoked_at:        datetime
    revoked_by:        text(max: 320)
    revocation_reason: text(max: 512)
                       @default("''")
                       @require(
                           "status != 'revoked' || size(revocation_reason) > 0",
                           "revoking an install credential requires a recorded reason"
                       )
}
